# Exercise: Measure L (Queue Depth)
#
# Little's Law: L = λW
#   L = the average number of items in the system at any moment
#
# When arrivals outpace service, items queue up — L grows. Setup:
# one slow worker (20ms per item) and a burst of 4 items sent
# back-to-back. Because the sends are near-instant and the worker
# can only chew through one item every 20ms, all 4 items pile up
# in the system before the first one finishes.
#
# To MEASURE L, we maintain an in-flight counter ourselves:
#   - increment on each `chan.send`
#   - decrement on each `chan.receive`
# After the last send (before any receives), the counter is at its
# peak — that peak IS an L sample, and it's the easiest to capture.
#
# Stack-shuffling notes:
#   `N pick` copies the element N down to the top, non-destructively.
#   - To send: push the value first, giving ( w r counter v ). The
#     value is at depth 0, counter at 1, results at 2, work at 3.
#     `3 pick` brings work to the top for `chan.send`.
#   - To receive: nothing pushed first, so on ( w r peak counter )
#     the counter is at depth 0, peak at 1, results at 2. `2 pick`
#     brings results to the top for `chan.receive`.
#
# Your task: bracket the burst with a counter, capture the peak
# value, then drain the queue. Leave the peak on top.
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: test-queue-depth ( -- )
    chan.make   # work
    chan.make   # results

    # Spawn 1 slow worker, unrolled to handle 4 items at 20ms each
    [ over chan.receive drop 20 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ over chan.send drop
      over chan.receive drop 20 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ over chan.send drop
      over chan.receive drop 20 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ over chan.send drop
      over chan.receive drop 20 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ over chan.send drop
      drop drop ] strand.spawn drop

    # Stack going in: ( work results )
    # Your code here:
    # - push a 0 — that's our starting in-flight counter
    # - for each of 4 items (1, 2, 3, 4): push the value, then
    #   `3 pick` for work, then `chan.send drop`, then `1 i.+` to
    #   bump the counter
    # - `dup` the counter to save the peak value
    # - receive 4 results, decrementing the counter each time
    #   (`2 pick` for results — counter and peak sit above it)
    # - drop the (now-zero) counter, then clean the two channels
    #   off the stack with `nip nip`
    # Stack coming out: ( peak-L )
    drop drop 0

    # Do not edit below this line
    # Peak L should be at least 3 — proves the queue built up
    3 i.>= test.assert
;
